### OpenCode's cost

**Correction ({{ oc_fix.found }}): OpenCode's cost in this run is too low, probably by {{ "%.2f" | format(oc_fix.factor_low) }}–{{ "%.2f" | format(oc_fix.factor_high) }}×.** The {{ oc.avg_cost_per_question | money }} a question recorded above is OpenCode's own estimate: its tokens at the OpenRouter catalog price of GLM 5.3, which is the cheapest of the providers OpenRouter routes that model to. OpenRouter billed whichever provider served each request, and OpenCode never sees that bill. RAGBench's own GLM 5.3 calls in the [model comparison](#open-weight-generators) record what OpenRouter billed: {{ "%.2f" | format(oc_fix.factor_low) }}× their tokens at the catalog price in `agent_search_tools` and {{ "%.2f" | format(oc_fix.factor_high) }}× in `hybrid_rerank_default`. At those factors OpenCode cost about {{ oc_low | money(3) }}–{{ oc_high | money(3) }} a question, {{ "%.0f" | format(oc_low / hr_h.avg_cost_per_question) }}–{{ "%.0f" | format(oc_high / hr_h.avg_cost_per_question) }}× `hybrid_rerank_default` and more than Claude Code ({{ cc.avg_cost_per_question | money }}). Its answer scores are unaffected. OpenCode also sends no routing preferences, so its requests did not carry the config's `openrouter.provider.data_collection: deny`.

The fix is in this release: with an `openrouter:` model, the `opencode` preset sends OpenCode's requests through a local relay that records OpenRouter's bill and applies the config's routing ([`openrouter_relay.py`](../../src/ragbench/rag_systems/openrouter_relay.py)). The figures above are this run's and were not re-measured; the correction is recorded with its source in [`corrections.json`](multihop-rag-harnesses-run/corrections.json).

